4.1 The Island is using the World Bank (WB) methodology for its risk assessment work except for PF where the Royal United Services Institute methodology is used. The WB methodology was used for the 2015 and 2020 ML/TF NRAs.

4.2 This Banking Risk Assessment uses the WB ML Risk Assessment Tool which aims to:

- identify the overall vulnerability of the Banking sector;

- identify bank products/services/channels with high vulnerability;

- prioritise action plans that will strengthen AML controls in the Banking sector

- 4.3 Data, statistics, and information used for completing the Risk Assessment Tool modules, as well as findings, interpretation, and judgment under the scope of the Risk Assessment, completely belong to the IOM authorities and do not reflect the views of the World Bank Group.

- 9.3.5 While these activities may originate outside the IOM, they can still pose a risk to IOM banks - particularly where funds are routed through a variety of overseas routes, including non-banking channels, before entering the domestic banking system. Such accounts are frequently exploited by foreign criminal networks and professional money laundering operations to disguise illicit funds as legitimate business activity. In some cases, proceeds are withdrawn in cash or transferred internationally via money service businesses, complicating detection and recovery efforts.
- 9.3.6 The increase in the variety of non-banking channels outside the Island available to IOM-based individuals or businesses to transmit money - e.g. payment service providers (including transacting via virtual assets), and overseas challenger banks - all create a wider risk. However, domestically, established international and UK high street banks continue to be central to the IOM's international financial services offering.
- 9.3.7 Where predicate offences occur outside the IOM but the criminal proceeds may flow through or be connected to the IOM banking system, the core threats (in order of prevalence) arise from the following:
- fraud (including fraud against customers of IOM banks; e.g. APP fraud);
- tax and excise evasion (or aggressive avoidance);
- drug-related (including organised crime);
- other organised crime activity;
- bribery and corruption (including pertaining to foreign PEPs)

IOM banks provide a range of banking services to:

- charities and clubs/associations;

- domestic-focused businesses;

- corporate and trust vehicles managed/administered by registered TCSPs;

- internationally-focused corporations (for example, providing banking services as part of wider group connections); and

- banks, the government, and other financial institutions (institutional business).

In addition to the same inherent features as outlined for domestic businesses (noting cash activity will be less of a feature), further vulnerabilities are prevalent due to the greater prominence of:

- non-face-to-face business and/or use of third parties/ introducers;

- higher levels of international transactions (transaction size and frequency);

- complex ownership structures, including clients introduced, and managed, by TCSPs;

- potential for foreign PEP connections (and corresponding challenges to establish source of wealth)

These entities will also often have no physical presence in the Island outside of being managed by a TCSP; therefore, it is more challenging for banks to understand business models and transaction rationale. These factors can lead to higher-risk customers being increasingly prevalent within the sector.
